# Rust build artifacts
/target
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# corpus/ is now ONLY what we cannot commit — third-party code and machine-local config:
#   corpus/oss/       — dogfood corpus checkouts (cloned OSS repos with embedded .git;
#                       re-acquisition commands live in the internal context docs)
#   corpus/*.json*    — local e2e configs/overlays (machine-specific absolute paths)
# The line that used to be here ignored `/corpus` wholesale, which also swallowed the labeled
# detection benchmark. That fixture is 100% our own synthetic code — no third-party source, no
# embedded .git, ~150 files / ~120 KB — so "we cannot ship it" was never true of it. It moved to
# `cases/` (tracked, committed) 2026-07-26, moved out of `examples/` 2026-07-28; the split line is now authorship, not location:
# what WE wrote lives under examples/ and ships, what SOMEONE ELSE wrote stays here and does not.
/corpus/oss/
/corpus/*.json
/corpus/*.jsonc

# The ONE file the promotion above could not carry, and it is not an authorship question.
# `security/vendor-token-committed` only fires on a CONTIGUOUS live vendor-token literal, so its
# benchmark fixture must contain a real `sk_live_`-shaped string — which is exactly what GitHub push
# protection rejects at push time, and what scripts/check-vendor-token-literals.sh (no escape hatch,
# by design) blocks at commit time. Verified 2026-07-26 rather than assumed: splitting the literal
# (`'sk_li' + 've_...'`) makes BOTH `vendor-token-committed` and `hardcoded-secret` go silent — the
# matcher is a text shape with no concat folding — so the `concat!` convention that fixes this for
# Rust fixtures has no TypeScript equivalent. That guard's own header already prescribed this outcome
# for a non-Rust file: keep the fixture out of the tracked tree.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, stated because it is a real hole and not a detail: scoring the benchmark reports 2 false
# negatives on `api/services/be-security.hardcoded-secret.ts:3` and `benchmark.mjs` exits nonzero. This
# is NOT limited to a fresh clone — measured 2026-07-27: the analyzer honors .gitignore
# (crates/engine/src/pipeline/walking.rs, ancestor ignores included), so THIS LINE ITSELF is what hides
# the fixture, and recreating the file at that path changes nothing. The same corpus copied outside the
# repo, fixture present, scores 143/143 with zero FP. The symptom is disclosed where it appears rather
# than only here: the expectation is listed in EXPECTED.jsonc's `untracked` array, and benchmark.mjs
# prints that reason next to the FN it produces. The rule itself is NOT unmeasured —
# rules/dsl/security/vendor_token_committed.rs covers it with split literals written to a temp dir at
# test time. Open question for whoever wires the CI gate: synthesize this file OUTSIDE the ignored path
# before a run (copying the corpus to a temp dir works — an in-place recreate does not), or drop the
# expectation and accept the coverage loss.
/cases/trees/api-be/services/be-security.hardcoded-secret.ts

# zzop's own derived artifacts inside an analyzed tree — today `.zzop/cache/` (the default `cacheDir`,
# `zzop_cache::DEFAULT_CACHE_DIR`), created on the first run of any zero-config analysis, this repo's
# own dogfooding included. Pure derived state: safe to delete, regenerated next run.
#
# `**/.zzop/`, not the root-anchored `/.zzop/` it replaced: the default `cacheDir` resolves against the
# BASE DIRECTORY of the run, and a config-free multi-path run (`zzop cross a b c`, the MCP `paths` mode)
# gives every path its own base — so cache directories appear beside each analyzed package, not only at
# the repo root, and root-anchoring left those untracked-but-unignored.
#
# Still never a `zzop*` glob: the user-authored `zzop/` (custom rule packs, adapter overlays) is ONE
# character away and must stay committed. This one line replaces the removed JS CLI's `zzop-report.*/` +
# `/zzop-reports/` pair — that CLI is gone and nothing writes those names any more.
**/.zzop/

# Working scratch: measurement harness + per-agent task files + `runs/<label>/` outputs.
# Local-only by the same rule as /corpus — never shipped. Listed explicitly because a bare
# `git add -A` at commit time otherwise sweeps agent task files into a public commit
# (`git check-ignore` is NOT a reliable check here — it reports a match against a blank
# .gitignore line for this path; `git add -A -n` is the one that tells the truth).
/scratchpad

